.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why RuboCop, and Why Opt-In
  :markdown
    Three things a linter buys a team: **consistency** (one style across
    every file, regardless of who wrote it), **fewer review nits** (the
    machine says "double quotes" so a human never has to), and **real bug
    detection** — the `Lint` department catches unused variables, unreachable
    code, forgotten `debugger` calls, and shadowed arguments before a reviewer
    ever sees them. Most of it is autocorrectable, so the cost of adopting a
    rule is one commit.

    The default RuboCop experience is the opposite of that promise: hundreds
    of cops on at once, thousands of offences on day one, and a team that
    learns to ignore the output. So we configure it **opt-in**.
    `DisabledByDefault: true` turns everything off, and each cop is enabled
    deliberately, usually because a code review just argued about it. The
    linter then only enforces conventions the team has actually agreed to —
    which is what makes it trustworthy enough to gate CI.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "install", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Installation
  :markdown
    RuboCop is a development tool, not a runtime dependency, so it goes in
    the `:development` group with `require: false`. We pair it with
    `rubocop-rails` (Rails-aware cops) and, for apps with an RSpec suite,
    `rubocop-rspec`. `rubocop-performance` is worth adding once the code is
    in shape:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      group :development do
        gem "rubocop", require: false
        gem "rubocop-rails", require: false
        gem "rubocop-rspec", require: false
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Extensions are loaded through the `plugins:` key (RuboCop 1.72 and
    later; older extensions that don't ship as plugins still use
    `require:`). If your team runs several apps, `inherit_from:` accepts a
    URL, so one shared baseline can live in a repo and every app pulls it:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # .rubocop.yml
      plugins:
        - rubocop-rails
        - rubocop-rspec

      # Optional: share one baseline across apps. RuboCop caches the download.
      # inherit_from:
      #   - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/your-org/standards/main/rubocop.yml

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "running", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Running It
  :markdown
    The everyday commands, from safest to most powerful. `-a` applies only
    autocorrections RuboCop knows are behaviour-preserving; `-A` also applies
    the *unsafe* ones, which are usually right but deserve a look at the diff
    before you commit:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle exec rubocop
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle exec rubocop -a
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle exec rubocop -A
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle exec rubocop --only Style/GuardClause app/

.mt-4.prose
  :markdown
    `--only` is how you adopt a cop: enable it, run just that cop with `-a`
    across the codebase, review, and commit the result on its own. For a
    fast local loop on a big app, lint only what you changed:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
    git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AMR origin/main... -- '*.rb' | xargs -r bundle exec rubocop

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "baseline", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. The Baseline Configuration
  :markdown
    This is the `.rubocop.yml` we start every app from. It is short on
    purpose: the whole `Lint` department (real bugs, near-zero false
    positives), a handful of `Style` and `Layout` cops that encode
    conventions we have already written down, and the two Rails cops that
    catch security and data-integrity mistakes. Everything else is off until
    someone makes the case for it.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # .rubocop.yml
      plugins:
        - rubocop-rails

      AllCops:
        # Opt-in: every cop is off until it is enabled below.
        DisabledByDefault: true
        # Review new cops deliberately rather than inheriting each release's.
        NewCops: disable
        SuggestExtensions: false
        TargetRubyVersion: 3.4
        Exclude:
          - "bin/**/*"
          - "db/**/*"
          - "node_modules/**/*"
          - "tmp/**/*"
          - "vendor/**/*"

      # --- Real bugs: the whole department, minus two that fight Rails idioms.
      Lint:
        Enabled: true
      Lint/UnusedMethodArgument:
        Enabled: false   # hook methods have fixed signatures
      Lint/ConstantDefinitionInBlock:
        Enabled: false   # specs and DSLs define constants in blocks

      # --- Conventions we have already agreed on.
      Layout/TrailingWhitespace:
        Enabled: true
      Style/FrozenStringLiteralComment:
        Enabled: true
        EnforcedStyle: always
      Style/StringLiterals:
        Enabled: true
        EnforcedStyle: double_quotes
      Style/HashSyntax:
        Enabled: true
        EnforcedStyle: ruby19
      Style/GuardClause:
        Enabled: true    # `return x if cond` over wrapping a method body in if/else
      Style/StringConcatenation:
        Enabled: true    # interpolation over `+`
      Naming/AccessorMethodName:
        Enabled: true    # no `get_` / `set_` prefixes on plain accessors
      Metrics/ParameterLists:
        Enabled: true
        CountKeywordArgs: false   # keyword args have no call-site ambiguity

      # --- Rails cops that catch real mistakes.
      Rails/OutputSafety:
        Enabled: true    # every `html_safe` / `raw` is a deliberate decision
      Rails/SkipsModelValidations:
        Enabled: true    # `update_column` & co. bypass validations on purpose — say so

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
