= doc_note(modifier: :tip, title: "Adopting a Cop", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    The unit of change is one cop per commit: enable it, `rubocop --only
    That/Cop -a`, review, commit. A PR that turns on five cops and touches
    two hundred files is unreviewable; five PRs that each turn on one are
    trivial.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "exceptions", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Exceptions
  :markdown
    A cop that is right 99% of the time is still wrong sometimes, and the
    answer is a scoped, explained disable — never turning the cop off
    globally. RuboCop's inline comments disable a cop for a range of lines;
    keep the range tight and put the reason in the surrounding comment. This
    is LocoMotion's own `set_component_name`, which really is a setter with
    an argument and not an accessor:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Sets the component name used in CSS generation.
      #
      # rubocop:disable Naming/AccessorMethodName
      def self.set_component_name(component_name)
        self.component_name = component_name
      end
      # rubocop:enable Naming/AccessorMethodName

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    For a single line, the trailing form `# rubocop:disable Cop/Name` on the
    line itself needs no matching `enable`. If the same cop needs disabling
    in a whole directory — generated code, a vendored file — use the cop's
    `Exclude:` in `.rubocop.yml` instead, with a comment saying why.

= doc_note(modifier: :note, title: "Adopting on an Existing Codebase", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    Unlike haml_lint (see the
    <a href="#{guide_path('component_linting')}#adopting" data-turbo="false">Component Linting guide</a>),
    RuboCop's `--auto-gen-config` is a real per-file ratchet: it writes
    `.rubocop_todo.yml` excluding today's offenders cop by cop, so new code
    is held to the rule while the backlog drains. With the opt-in baseline
    you will rarely need it — enabling cops one at a time *is* the ratchet.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "integration", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Editor, Hooks, and CI
  :markdown
    **Editor.** RuboCop ships a language server (`rubocop --lsp`), and
    [Ruby LSP](https://github.com/Shopify/ruby-lsp) picks RuboCop up
    automatically when it is in the `Gemfile` — offences appear inline and
    format-on-save applies the safe autocorrections. No editor plugin
    configuration is needed beyond installing Ruby LSP.

    **Hooks.** Run it on the files you touched (the `git diff` one-liner
    above) as a pre-commit hook, or let an agent-side runner do it: this
    repository runs `bundle exec rubocop` as a
    [Highball](https://github.com/profoundry-us/highball) check on every
    editing turn.

    **CI.** Run `bundle exec rubocop` as its own job — it takes seconds and
    needs no database — and make it a **hard gate**. That is the payoff of
    the opt-in baseline: every enabled cop is one the team agreed to, so a
    red check is never "the linter being fussy", it is a convention being
    broken. LocoMotion's own CI runs it as a dedicated job:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # .github/workflows/ci.yml
      rubocop:
        runs-on: ubuntu-latest
        steps:
          - uses: actions/checkout@v7
          - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@v1
            with:
              bundler-cache: true
          - run: bundle exec rubocop

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "success", class: "scroll-mt-24" } Success!
  :markdown
    Your linter enforces exactly the conventions your team has agreed to,
    everywhere Ruby is written. Before you move on:

